{% extends "_layouts/default.njk" %}

{% block content %}
  <div class="nhsuk-grid-row">
    <div class="nhsuk-grid-column-two-thirds">
      <h1 class="nhsuk-heading-xl">Non-routine doses</h1>
      <p>Some children may get an extra dose of MMR that is outside the
      <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/routine-childhood-immunisation-schedule/routine-childhood-immunisations-from-1-july-2025-babies-born-from-1-july-2024">UK Health Security Agency (UKHSA) routine schedule</a>.</p>
      <p>If a child is vaccinated before their 1st birthday (for example, because of a local outbreak or for travel) it will contribute to their immunity but they will still need 2 more doses to be fully protected.</p>
      <p>Similarly, if a child has their 2nd dose before the age of 18 months, or less than 4 weeks after an earlier vaccination, it will contribute to their immunity for travel reasons or a local outbreak, but they will need a 3rd dose to be fully protected.</p>
      <p>There is no harm in having extra doses of the MMR vaccine. If a child is already protected from earlier doses and has an extra dose, their immune system will recognize and quickly destroy the vaccine viruses. See the <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/mmr-for-all-general-leaflet/mmr-for-all-general-guide">UK Health Security Agency MMR guidance</a>.</p>
      <p>In rare cases, some children who have had 2 doses may need extra doses for medical reasons, for example, if they’ve had a blood transfusion, a stem cell transplant or chemotherapy.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endblock %}
